1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:05,517
This video shows how caching works in doeff, a library for algebraic effects in Python.

2
00:00:05,517 --> 00:00:08,050
We follow one function, with manual cache.

3
00:00:08,050 --> 00:00:12,650
It looks a value up by a key, and works it out only when the key is missing.

4
00:00:13,200 --> 00:00:20,700
The cache system of doeff. A program asks for a value it has worked out before, and a handler keeps it.

5
00:00:21,250 --> 00:00:28,550
Two effects do the asking. Cache Get asks for the value under a key. Cache Put stores one.

6
00:00:29,250 --> 00:00:33,950
Which cache answers them is not in the program. It is up to the handler around it.

7
00:00:36,250 --> 00:00:42,100
The first call. Cache Get goes out to the handler, and the cache has nothing under my key.

8
00:00:42,750 --> 00:00:48,450
So the handler throws a key error into the program, right at the yield where it was waiting.

9
00:00:49,250 --> 00:00:51,600
The program catches it, does the work,

10
00:00:51,600 --> 00:00:56,300
which takes four hundred milliseconds, and stores the answer with Cache Put.

11
00:00:57,250 --> 00:01:03,450
The second call. This time the handler finds my key, and resumes the program with forty-two.

12
00:01:04,750 --> 00:01:10,650
Half a millisecond instead of four hundred. The log shows a miss, then a hit.

13
00:01:11,250 --> 00:01:18,400
Cache Put can carry a policy: a time to live, a lifecycle, a storage, and metadata.

14
00:01:19,250 --> 00:01:25,100
The policy travels inside the effect, so the handler receives it together with the value.

15
00:01:25,750 --> 00:01:32,350
A persistent lifecycle resolves to disk storage. With no hints, the entry belongs in memory.

16
00:01:33,250 --> 00:01:36,650
These are hints, and the handler decides what they mean.

17
00:01:36,650 --> 00:01:44,050
The in-memory handler keeps every entry the same way: one with a time to live of a tenth of a second is still there later.

18
00:01:45,250 --> 00:01:50,350
The page's API example. fetch user is called twice, for the same user.

19
00:01:51,250 --> 00:01:58,050
The first call misses, sends the request, and stores the response. The second is served from the cache.

20
00:02:00,250 --> 00:02:07,200
Here the handler answer from memory decides what Get Json means, and it got exactly one request.

21
00:02:07,750 --> 00:02:12,700
Now the same program, run twice, under different cache handlers.

22
00:02:13,250 --> 00:02:19,350
The in-memory handler starts empty in every run, so both runs do the work.

23
00:02:20,250 --> 00:02:27,000
The SQLite handler keeps the cache in a file. The second run is a hit, in two milliseconds.

24
00:02:28,250 --> 00:02:36,600
With no cache handler at all, Cache Get is unhandled. The program never chose a cache. The handler does.

25
00:02:37,150 --> 00:02:41,150
Caching in doeff: the program asks and stores through effects,

26
00:02:41,150 --> 00:02:44,950
and the handler you install decides where the answers live.
